.TH TIMESYNC 8
.SH NAME
timesync \- set the clock from a GPS receiver
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B timesync
[
.B -n
.I samples
] [
.B -o
.I ms
] [
.B -t
.I ms
] [
.B -m
.I seconds
] [
.B -p
.I seconds
] [
.I timefile
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Timesync
sets this host's clock from the time a GPS receiver gives, through the
.B time
file of
.IR gpsfs (4)
(default
.BR /mnt/gps/time ;
if that is not mounted here, the posted
.B #sgps/fs
is mounted in a name space of the command's own, and with
.B -p
mounted again when the
.I gpsfs
that posted it has gone and another been started).
A host clock that cannot be read
.RB ( /dev/time )
is an error, and nothing is set.
It is for a host with no network time, as
.IR sntp (8)
needs, and no clock of its own that keeps time: a board in the field.
.PP
The time file pairs the time of the last fix with this host's clock
when the fix's first sentence began to arrive.
A sentence arrives after the time it gives, never before, and by a
delay that varies; so of
.I samples
fixes (5, each one second's), the one whose offset is largest was the
least delayed, and its offset is taken, as Plan 9's
.I timesync
does: the largest of those within two seconds of the samples' median,
which must be more than half of them, so that one sample that is not
the receiver's does not carry the clock.
Only fixes marked
.B sync=yes
count: never a recording's, a feed's, or one whose time this host
stamped (a receiver giving none, as GDL 90 and Garmin's Aviation
output do), nor a network source's unless
.IR gpsfs 's
.B source
command said
.BR sync .
.PP
A time is not taken on one say-so, since the clock decides which
certificates are in date.
A GPS time before this system was built (the later of the day
.I timesync
was written and the kernel's build date) is no time, and is refused.
A clock that has been set, one already past that date as a host's or
an RTC's is, is stepped by no more than
.B -m
.I seconds
(86400, a day: such a clock is off by its drift or by a time zone, and
more than that is another date);
.B "-m 0"
allows any step.
A clock never set, as a board's with no RTC is when it starts, is
stepped by whatever brings it to the time.
A record is used once: none that is not later, by the receiver's time,
than the last a result was made from.
.PP
What is left is the receiver's own delay between the second and the
sentence giving it, tens to hundreds of milliseconds by receiver;
.B -o
adds a correction in milliseconds, measured once against a better
clock.
.PP
If the clock is off by more than
.B -t
milliseconds (200) it is stepped, through
.B /dev/time
.RI ( cons (3)).
.I Timesync
says by how much, and whether it set the clock or left it; a time it
refuses is an error, saying why, and the clock is left.
With
.B -p
it samples every
.I seconds
and steps when it must, saying so only then.
.SH EXAMPLES
On a Raspberry Pi with a receiver on its serial port and no network:
.IP
.EX
gpsfs source '#t/eia0' 9600
timesync -p 600 &
.EE
.PP
From a marine gateway that sends NMEA to UDP port 10110, on a network
whose every host is trusted with the clock:
.IP
.EX
gpsfs source 'udp!*!10110' sync
timesync
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/timesync.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR gpsfs (4),
.IR cons (3),
.IR sntp (8)
.SH BUGS
NMEA gives time to tens of milliseconds at best.
A receiver's pulse per second, which would give it to microseconds, is
not read.
.PP
The clock is stepped, not slewed.
.PP
GPS time is not authenticated: a receiver believes the strongest
signal, and a network source whoever speaks.
The bounds above limit what a false time can do to a clock already
set; they do not make the time true.
